introduction:
Divers working in confined spaces, such as underwater cave systems [1], pipelines [2], coral reef canyons [3], or around unexploded ordnance [4], must often maintain a safe operating distance from the surrounding walls or objects.
In the case of cave diving, for example, avoiding cave walls helps divers maintain optimal visibility conditions by avoiding collisions with the surrounding enclosure.
This minimizes stirring up deposited sediment [5], which is a leading cause of diver disorientation-induced fatalities in cave diving accidents [6].
Maintaining safe standoff distances for divers during coral reef explorations is also important, since ocean currents, other divers, or marine obstacles can inadvertently cause divers to touch coral [3], which devastates coral health.

In this paper, we introduce RADMCS (Robotic Assisted Diver Movement in Confined Spaces) with the goal of addressing these distance-maintaining challenges.
RADMCS is an easy-to-deploy wearable robotic solution that assists in maintaining a preset standoff distance from encompassing walls or objects by providing force-feedback to the diver when they get too close to such objects.
The force-feedback mechanism is driven by monocular depth estimation techniques that estimate the distance between the robot’s cameras and the surrounding structure, and the robot uses its thrusters in response to this perceived distance.
Fig. 1 shows the haptic feedback mechanism working in response to the distance between the surrounding reef and the robot’s cameras.
Because the robot is affixed to the diver, the actuation, in turn, perturbs the diver’s movements. RADMCS integrates on the diver’s existing equipment by strapping onto the scuba diving air tank.
This eliminates the need for complex exoskeleton style suits or augmented swimming solutions that require additional training or instrumentation.
It is self-contained and does not interfere with the diver’s natural body movements beyond the perturbative feedback provided to the diver.

Our contributions are the

introduction of a novel, wearable underwater robotic system for movement assistance;

presentation of systematic testing of threshold sensitivity (TS) for haptic feedback with human participants in an underwater study in ocean and closed-water environments;

exploration of closed-water experiments of simulated wall-following to gauge the robot’s effectiveness at directing human participants;

demonstration of form, fit, and function of the current design in open-water field experiments for TS and maneuverability near coral reefs.

1. introduction of a novel, wearable underwater robotic system for movement assistance;

2. presentation of systematic testing of threshold sensitivity (TS) for haptic feedback with human participants in an underwater study in ocean and closed-water environments;

3. exploration of closed-water experiments of simulated wall-following to gauge the robot’s effectiveness at directing human participants;

4. demonstration of form, fit, and function of the current design in open-water field experiments for TS and maneuverability near coral reefs.

Construction remains one of the least automated sectors despite persistent labor shortages, hazardous working conditions, and declining productivity [1]. This stands in sharp contrast to more structured environments like manufacturing [2] and warehousing [3], where advances in robotics and artificial intelligence have automated many core tasks. A key barrier to automating construction labor is the dynamic and unstructured nature of construction sites, requiring robots that navigate human-scale spaces and perform diverse tasks that combine mobility with dexterous manipulation [1]. Existing construction automation has largely focused on task-specific systems, either in controlled, off-site environments where robots perform repetitive tasks such as frame prefabrication [4] [5], or directly on construction sites for activities such as bricklaying, drywall installation, and robotic assembly [5] [6]. This motivates the development of robotic systems that can operate directly in construction sites while supporting a broader range of tasks.

Humanoids are a natural fit for on-site construction work because their morphology matches spaces and tools built for humans. They can navigate tight spaces, climb steps, and use common tools without site modification or task-specific automation [1]. This allows humanoids to address a broad range of tasks with a single embodiment. While the existing humanoid models may not be optimal for every task, they offer reach and mobility no fixed or wheeled system can match [1]. However, reliable, full autonomy of humanoid robots remains out of the reach in the noise and clutter of a real construction site [1].

Teleoperation offers a practical near-term path: skilled operators control robots remotely, reducing physical strain and hazard to workers while generating high-quality demonstration data for training future autonomous models. Manual material handling is a leading cause of musculoskeletal disorders [7], and over 1,000 construction workers die on the job each year in the United States [8]. These conditions compound a persistent labor shortage, as an aging workforce approaches retirement while the physical toll deters new entrants [9]. By transferring strain and hazard to the robot while leveraging the operator’s expertise, teleoperation can improve worker safety and career longevity. Moreover, recent whole-body teleoperation systems have shown that expert demonstrations are key to training autonomous vision-language-action (VLA) models [10, 11, 12]. However, existing whole-body teleoperation systems have been evaluated primarily on household and laboratory tasks, leaving their effectiveness for construction work largely unexplored.

In this work, we develop a teleoperation system for a humanoid construction robot from a seated workstation using a Meta Quest 3 extended-reality (XR) headset for head and hand tracking and GLYDR foot pedals for locomotion control. We then deploy a Unitree G1 humanoid robot on an active construction site and perform a feasibility study on two high-importance construction tasks selected from the O*NET occupational database [13]. Our key contributions are:

A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

Deployment on an active construction site;

A preliminary evaluation of teleoperation performance on two representative construction tasks.

1. A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

2. Deployment on an active construction site;

3. A preliminary evaluation of teleoperation performance on two representative construction tasks.
